        "bodyTr": "Unification Minister Jung Dong-young's remarks are obscuring North Korea's responsibility for provocations and straining trust in the Korea-U.S. alliance. When South Korea's UN Ambassador Cha Ji-hoon stated at the UN General Assembly on the 8th that North Korea's placement of mines south of the military demarcation line violated the armistice agreement and demanded an apology, the North Korean representative countered by citing remarks Jung made in the National Assembly. Despite the Joint Chiefs of Staff and United Nations Command confirming North Korea's wrongdoing, Minister Jung characterized the incident in which three Korean service members were injured as an \"accident\" and stated, \"It cannot yet be specified as a mine attack.\" North Korea seized upon this as grounds for evading responsibility. Kim Yo-jong, vice director of the North Korean Workers' Party, even mocked the minister, saying he had \"admitted it is difficult to conclusively determine this was an intentional North Korean attack.\" A senior government official who should have condemned North Korea's provocation instead provided North Korea with ammunition for rebuttal.\n\nThis is not the first time Minister Jung has stirred controversy over sensitive issues. His stance regarding North Korea's \"two-state theory\" is also concerning. Minister Jung refers to inter-Korean relations using North Korea's terminology of \"Jo-Han relations\" and appears to be responding to North Korea's \"two states\" doctrine through his words and actions. On independent sanctions against North Korea, he has revealed differing views with the Foreign Ministry, failing to maintain a consistent voice in inter-agency policy coordination. In March, Minister Jung publicly mentioned uranium enrichment facilities in North Korean territories during a National Assembly hearing. Immediately afterward, restrictions on Korea-U.S. intelligence sharing began and have now continued for more than half a year. Minister Jung has claimed his remarks were based on publicly available information, and President Lee Jae-myung subsequently defended him by saying it was \"already widely known fact.\" However, the United States has maintained its position that Minister Jung publicly disclosed in the National Assembly confidential intelligence that America had painstakingly accumulated and provided to its ally. Experts cannot overlook the difference between information known among specialists and a minister making pronouncements in the National Assembly. According to sources in relevant fields, the problem lies not only in the remarks themselves but in the response afterward, which has invited American distrust and contributed significantly to cracks in intelligence cooperation between allies.\n\nThe minister of unification bears responsibility not only for improving inter-Korean relations but also, as an important member of the National Security Council, for national security and alliance trust. It is questionable whether Minister Jung, who obscures North Korea's responsibility for provocations, gives the impression of defending North Korea, and stands at the center of repeated controversies over remarks and policy confusion, should continue to hold this weighty position as unification minister.",
